+---
+id: dl-004-applymergepatch-semantics-rfc-7386
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyMergePatch semantics (RFC 7386)"
+status: pending
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold verbatim; `memory submit` replaces
+     these placeholder comments with real content and fills the required frontmatter fields. -->
+
+## Context
+
+Clients send partial updates as JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386). The library already follows the standards it implements, and `applyPatch` neither mutates its inputs nor shares structure with them.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyMergePatch` follows the RFC 7386 MergePatch pseudocode: a non-object patch (including `null`, arrays, scalars) replaces the target; an object patch merges member by member; a `null` member removes that member; arrays are replaced whole.
+- A non-object target is treated as `{}` for an object patch, and nested object patches applied to missing members are merged into `{}` so nulls inside them are dropped (appendix A: `{}` + `{"a":{"bb":{"ccc":null}}}` gives `{"a":{"bb":{}}}`).
+- Inputs are never mutated; the result is built from copies (`structuredClone`), as in `applyPatch`.
+- Members are written with `defineProperty` (as `setMember`) so a `__proto__` member is plain data, never a prototype change. Target member order is kept, new members are appended.
+- Invalid-input errors are not defined by the RFC: every JSON value is a valid patch, so the function does not throw.
+
+## Consequences
+
+A `null` patch returns `null`, so callers cannot express "set to null" with a merge patch (inherent to the RFC). Non-JSON values (functions, symbols) are rejected by `structuredClone`.
